    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I was a bit stuck on the stagnation pressure part. What happens if the tube gets blocked though? Like, if something gets inside it, how does that affect the airspeed reading specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question — and honestly, blocked pitot tubes are a serious concern, which is why preflight checks matter so much. If the pitot tube gets blocked but the drain hole stays open, the pressure gets trapped in the system, and the airspeed indicator reads zero because there's no dynamic pressure changes to measure. If the blockage is complete (both the tube and drain hole), the airspeed indicator acts like an altimeter — as you climb, the trapped pressure stays constant while static pressure drops, so the ASI reads higher and higher, and it reads lower than actual when descending. That's a big deal because pilots can be fooled into thinking they need to dive or climb when they shouldn't.",
      "source": "target",
